# Paddock runtime secrets. Copy to `paddock.env` (gitignored) and fill in.
# NEVER commit real tokens.

# Claude auth. Use ONE of these:
#   - CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN  for a Claude Max/Pro subscription (runtime: cli)
#   - ANTHROPIC_API_KEY        for pay-as-you-go API billing (runtime: sdk)
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Optional: a fine-grained GitHub PAT so keepers can push to their repos.
# Scope it to the minimum set of repos — that is the blast radius if leaked.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ---- Optional: Management API (remote MCP at /mcp) -------------------------
# One variable per management client, holding that client's bearer token. The
# token is REFERENCED from paddock.config.yaml (`ref: env:PADDOCK_MCP_TOKEN_…`)
# and must never be written into that file — it is git-tracked, so an inline
# value would leak into history. Paddock rejects an inline token outright.
#
# Mint one (>= 24 chars; the pdk_<instanceId>_ prefix binds it to THIS instance,
# so a copy is useless at any other Paddock):
#   printf 'pdk_%s_%s\n' myinstance "$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#
# The variable name is yours; it just has to match the `ref:` in the config.
# Leave it unset and the client is dropped at boot — with no clients, /mcp
# reverts to 404 and the endpoint does not exist. See ../README.md ("Remote
# MCP"), and heed the deploy-ordering warning there: /mcp is exempt from Basic
# Auth in this recipe, so only run it against Paddock v0.46.0+, which
# authenticates /mcp itself.
# PADDOCK_MCP_TOKEN_LAPTOP=
